---
title: ElementField
description: Provides field context for a single element of an array — one row of a repeated group, with per-item errors and constraints.
---

<script>
	import LiveArrayDemo from "$lib/demos/live-array-demo.svelte";
</script>

`ElementField` is the array counterpart of [Field](/docs/components/field). It scopes context to one indexed path such as `urls[0]` or `contacts[2].email`, so the [Control](/docs/components/control) inside it gets that element's errors, constraints and value. Like `Field`, it renders no element of its own.

It must be nested inside an enclosing `Field` or [Fieldset](/docs/components/fieldset), which supplies the parent context.

## Live example

Add and remove rows, then submit empty values: every row keeps its own error region, and the rows are keyed by a stable id list rather than by object identity.

<LiveArrayDemo />

## Primitive arrays

For a primitive array (`string[]`, `number[]`), the control's `name` attribute renders as the repeated parent name — every `urls[0]`, `urls[1]` control submits as `urls` — so a native, JavaScript-free POST works. Key rows by your own stable ID list, not by array objects:

```svelte title="src/routes/links/links-form.svelte"
<script lang="ts">
	import { untrack } from "svelte";
	import { superForm, type SuperValidated } from "sveltekit-superforms";
	import { Control, ElementField, Fieldset, Label, Legend } from "@emmorts/formsnap";
	import type { LinksData } from "./schema.js";

	let { validated }: { validated: SuperValidated<LinksData> } = $props();

	const form = superForm(untrack(() => validated));
	const { form: formData } = form;
	const rowIds = $state($formData.urls.map((_, index) => index));
</script>

<form method="POST">
	<Fieldset {form} name="urls" fieldErrors>
		<Legend>Website URLs</Legend>
		{#each $formData.urls as _, index (rowIds[index])}
			<ElementField {form} name={`urls[${index}]` as `urls[${number}]`}>
				<Control>
					{#snippet children({ props })}
						<Label>URL {index + 1}</Label>
						<input {...props} bind:value={$formData.urls[index]} />
					{/snippet}
				</Control>
			</ElementField>
		{/each}
	</Fieldset>
	<button type="submit">Save</button>
</form>
```

The `` as `urls[${number}]` `` cast keeps the path within the `FormPathLeaves<T>` type that Superforms generates for the schema.

## Object arrays

Nested object paths need Superforms' `dataType: "json"` and its `enhance` action, because a native POST cannot express a nested path through repeated HTML names:

```svelte title="src/routes/contacts/contacts-form.svelte"
<script lang="ts">
	import { untrack } from "svelte";
	import { superForm, type SuperValidated } from "sveltekit-superforms";
	import { Control, ElementField, Fieldset, Label, Legend } from "@emmorts/formsnap";
	import type { ContactsData } from "./schema.js";

	let { validated }: { validated: SuperValidated<ContactsData> } = $props();

	const form = superForm(untrack(() => validated), { dataType: "json" });
	const { form: formData, enhance } = form;
</script>

<form method="POST" use:enhance>
	<Fieldset {form} name="contacts" fieldErrors>
		<Legend>Contacts</Legend>
		{#each $formData.contacts as _, index (index)}
			<ElementField
				{form}
				name={`contacts[${index}].email` as `contacts[${number}].email`}
				fieldErrors
			>
				<Control>
					{#snippet children({ props })}
						<Label>Contact {index + 1}</Label>
						<input {...props} bind:value={$formData.contacts[index].email} />
					{/snippet}
				</Control>
				{#snippet description()}Used for order updates.{/snippet}
			</ElementField>
		{/each}
	</Fieldset>
</form>
```

## Props

| Prop | Type | Default | Description |
| ---- | ---- | ------- | ----------- |
| `form` | `FsSuperForm<T, M>` | — (required) | The form object returned from `superForm`. Fixed for the mounted scope's lifetime. |
| `name` | `FormPathLeaves<T>` | — (required) | The indexed path to the element, e.g. `"urls[0]"` or `"contacts[2].email"`. |
| `children` | `Snippet<[{ value, errors, tainted, constraints }]`> | — | Element content; receives the element's field state. |
| `description` | `Snippet` | — | Owned description content rendered after the children. |
| `fieldErrors` | `boolean \| Snippet<[{ errors, errorProps }]>` | — | Owned error region; `true` renders default content, a snippet renders custom content. |
| `descriptionId` | `string` | generated | Override for the owned description ID; ignored without `description`. |
| `fieldErrorsId` | `string` | generated | Override for the owned error region ID; ignored without `fieldErrors`. |
| `fieldErrorsLive` | `"assertive" \| "polite" \| "off"` | `"assertive"` | Announcement policy for the owned error region. |

## Behavior notes

- **Requires a parent scope.** `ElementField` reads the enclosing `Field` or `Fieldset` context; outside one it throws. To replace the `form` instance, key the whole enclosing subtree — parent included — not just the element field.
- **Description and error inheritance.** When the element renders no description or error region of its own, it inherits the parent's associations — including an owned parent description — so one fieldset-level description can describe every row. A local owned or standalone container overrides the inherited one.
- **Constraints without indices.** Superforms constraints describe the array item schema, so bracketed indices are stripped when looking up `constraints`; the snippet receives the item's constraint object.
- **SSR-stable IDs.** Owned `descriptionId` and `fieldErrorsId` default to `$props.id()`-generated values, stable between server rendering and hydration, and unique per component instance — repeated rows never share targets.
- **Submission shape.** Primitive arrays submit as repeated parent names natively. Nested object/array submissions require `dataType: "json"`, `use:enhance`, and values bound to the form store; enhancement serializes that store. Some Superforms options that identify inputs by full path (such as `customValidity`) may also need an explicit leaf `name`.

## When to use the hooks instead

Inside a custom row component, `useFormField` reads the element's `errors`, `constraints`, `tainted`, `errorsId` and `descriptionId` from the `ElementField` context without rendering children inline. See [Custom controls](/docs/recipes/custom-controls).

